#: {{ux|en|Children, please join hands and form a '''circle'''.}}
## Any [[thin]] [[three-dimensional]] [[equivalent]] of the [[geometric]] figures.
##: {{ux|en|Cut a '''circle''' out of that sheet of metal.}}
## A [[curve]] that more or less [[form]]s part or all of a circle.
##: {{ux|en|The crank moves in a '''circle'''.}}
## {{lb|en|cricket}} A [[line]] comprising two [[semicircle]]s of 30 yards [[radius]] centred on the [[wicket]]s joined by straight lines [[parallel]] to the [[pitch]] used to [[enforce]] [[field restrictions]] in a [[one-day match]].
##: {{syn|en|30 yard circle|cricket circle|field circle|fielding circle}}
## A [[magic circle]].
### {{lb|en|Wicca}} A ritual circle that is [[cast]] three times [[deosil]] and closes three times [[widdershins]] either in the a3ir with a [[wand]] or literally with stones or other items used for [[worship]].
## {{lb|en|South Africa|Philippines|India|Dundee}} A [[traffic circle]] or [[roundabout]].
##: {{syn|en|rotary|roundabout|traffic circle}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=2011| author=Charles E. Webb| title=Downfall and Freedom| page=120| passage=He arrived at the lakefront and drove around the '''circle''' where the amusement park and beach used to be when he was a kid{{nb...}}}}
## {{lb|en|jewelry|clothing}} A [[headband]] or [[diadem]].
## A [[road]] with a curve in it or that ends in a circle, e.g., a [[cul-de-sac]].
##: {{ux|en|I live on Grover '''Circle'''.}}
## {{lb|en|theater}} A curved level of a [[balcony]] of [[seat]]s in a [[theater]].
## {{lb|en|astronomy}} An instrument of observation, whose [[graduated]] limb consists of an entire circle. When fixed to a wall in an observatory, it is called a ''mural circle''; when mounted with a telescope on an axis and in Y's, in the plane of the meridian, a ''meridian'' or ''transit circle''; when involving the principle of reflection, like the sextant, a ''reflecting circle''; and when that of repeating an angle several times continuously along the graduated limb, a ''repeating circle''.
## {{lb|en|camping}} A [[campfire circle]].
## {{lb|en|dance}} A [[circle dance]].
# A specific [[group]] of persons; especially one who shares a common [[interest]].
#: {{ux|en|I need an in to start hanging out with that '''circle'''.}}
#: {{syn|en|circle of friends|crowd|bunch|gang|group|social circle}}
#: {{hypo|en|babysitting circle|family circle|inner circle|moral circle|prayer circle}}
#: {{co|en|literary '''circle'''}}
#* {{RQ:Macaulay Goldsmith|passage=As his name gradually became known, the '''circle''' of his acquaintance widened.}}
#* {{RQ:Besant Ivory Gate|III
|passage=At half-past nine on this Saturday evening, the [[parlour]] of the Salutation Inn, High Holborn, contained most of its customary visitors.{{...}}In former days every [[tavern]] of [[repute]] kept such a room for its own select '''circle''', a club, or society, of habitués, who met every [[evening]], for a pipe and a cheerful glass.}}
#* {{RQ:Chambers Younger Set|VI|passage=“I don't mean all of your friends—only a small proportion—which, [[however]], connects your '''circle''' with that deadly, idle, [[brainless]] bunch—the insolent chatterers at the opera, the [[gorged]] [[dowager]]s,{{nb...}}, the [[jewel]]led animals whose moral code is the code of the barnyard—!"}}
#* {{RQ:Williams Velveteen Rabbit|passage=The Rabbit could not claim to be a model of anything, for he didn’t know that real rabbits existed; he thought they were all stuffed with [[sawdust]] like himself, and he understood that sawdust was quite out-of-date and should never be mentioned in modern '''circles'''.}}
## The people who associate with and follow someone; one's [[inner circle]].
##: {{ux|en|I don't trust anyone in his '''circle'''.}}
##: {{syn|en|entourage|posse|retinue}}
## {{lb|en|Japanese fiction}} {{syn of|en|doujin circle}}.
# A real or figurative [[cycle]].
#: {{ux|en|the '''circle''' of the wheel}}
#: {{ux|en|We've come full '''circle'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|We've been talking in '''circles'''.}}
#: {{co|en|[[circle of life]]}}
## A series ending where it begins, and repeating [[itself]].
##* {{RQ:Dryden Georgics|2|passage=Thus in a '''circle''' runs the [[peasant]]'s pain.}}
## {{senseid|en|logic}}{{lb|en|logic}} A form of [[argument]] in which two or more unproved statements are used to prove each other; inconclusive reasoning.
##* {{RQ:Glanvill Vanity of Dogmatizing|chapter=XVIII|page=171|passage=That heavy Bodies deſcend by ''gravity'', is no better an account then we might expect from a ''Ruſtick'': and again; that ''Gravity'' is a ''quality'' whereby an heavy body deſcends, is an impertinent '''''Circle''''', and teacheth nothing.}}
## Indirect form of words; [[circumlocution]].
#* {{RQ:Jonson Alchemist
|passage=Has he given the lie, / In '''circle''', or oblique, or semicircle.}}
# A [[territorial]] [[division]] or district.
#: {{hypo|en|imperial circle}}
#: {{co|en|the '''circles''' of Hell}}
#: {{ux|en|The ten '''Circles''' of the Holy Roman Empire were those principalities or provinces which had seats in the German Diet.}}
## {{lb|en|music}} An area near a concert set aside for aggressive [[mosh]]ing.
##: {{syn|en|circle pit|mosh circle|pit|mosh pit}}
## {{lb|en|figurative|by extension}} An area of [[impact]] or [[possibility]].
##: {{ux|en|within the '''circle''' of probability}}
##: {{ux|en|These are the duties within the morning shift's '''circle''' of responsibility.}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} Compass; circuit; enclosure.
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare As You Like It|V|iv|passage=in the '''circle''' of this forest}}
# {{lb|en|in the plural}} A [[bagginess]] of the skin below the [[eye]]s from lack of [[sleep]].
#: {{ux|en|After working all night, she had '''circles''' under her eyes.}}
# {{lb|en|University of Warwick|_|slang}} An event at which [[circling]] takes place.

