===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The amount of money [[levy|levied]] for a [[service]].
#: {{ux|en|There will be a '''charge''' of five dollars.}}
#: {{ux|en|There is a cancellation '''charge''' if you withdraw your order.}}
# {{lb|en|military}} An [[attack]] in which combatants rush towards an enemy in an attempt to engage in close combat.
#: {{ux|en|[[Pickett's Charge|Pickett's '''Charge''']]; the {{w|Charge of the Light Brigade|'''Charge''' of the Light Brigade}}}}
# A [[forceful]] [[forward]] [[movement]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=March 2, 2011|author=Chris Whyatt|title=Arsenal 5 - 0 Leyton Orient| work=BBC| url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/eng_prem/9405635.stm| passage=Abou Diaby should have added Arsenal's fourth in the 50th minute after he danced round a host of defenders on a '''charge''' towards goal}}
# {{senseid|en|accusation}} An [[accusation]].
#: {{syn|en|count}}
## An official description (by the police or a court) of a crime that somebody may be guilty of.
##: {{ux|en|two '''charges''' of manslaughter}}
##* {{RQ:Noire Thug-A-Licious|page=108|passage="Ain't gone be no Rikers Island for you next time," I warned him. "You get tapped on another gun '''charge''' and you looking at some upstate time."}}
## An accusation by a person or organization.
##: {{syn|en|complaint}}
##: {{ux|en|That's a slanderous '''charge''' of abuse of trust.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2005|translator=w:Lesley Brown|author=w:Plato|title=Sophist|page=261a
|passage=we'll nail the sophist to it, if we can get him on that '''charge''';}}
##* {{quote-book|en|title=w:The Omnivore's Dilemma|author=w:Michael Pollan|publisher=The Penguin Press|year=2006|ISBN=1594200823|passage=A '''charge''' often leveled against organic agriculture is that it is more philosophy than science.|page=150}}
# {{lb|en|electromagnetism|chemistry|physics|countable|uncountable}} An [[electric charge]].
# The scope of someone's [[responsibility]].
#: {{ux|en|The child was in the nanny's '''charge'''.}}
#* '''1848''' April 24, {{w|John K. Kane}}, opinion, ''United States ''v.'' Hutchison'', as reported in ''The Pennsylvania law Journal'', June 1848 edition, as reprinted in, 1848,''The Pennsylvania Law Journal'' volume 7, page 366&nbsp;[http://books.google.com/books?id=Pz-TAAAAIAAJ&pg=PA366&dq=key]:
#*: {{quote|en|He had the key of a closet in which the moneys of this fund were kept, but the outer key of the vault, of which the closet formed part, was in the '''charge''' of another person.}}
# Someone or something [[entrust]]ed to one's care, such as a child to a babysitter or a student to a teacher.
#: {{ux|en|The child was a '''charge''' of the nanny.}}
# A [[load]] or [[burden]]; [[cargo]].
#: {{ux|en|The ship had a '''charge''' of colonists and their belongings.}}
# An [[instruction]].
#: {{ux|en|I gave him the '''charge''' to get the deal closed by the end of the month.}}
# {{lb|en|property law}} A [[mortgage]].
# {{lb|en|basketball}} An [[offensive foul]] in which the player with the ball moves into a stationary defender.
# {{lb|en|firearms}} A measured amount of [[powder]] and/or [[shot]] in a [[cartridge]].
# {{lb|en|by extension}} A measured amount of [[explosive]].
#* {{RQ:Beckett Watt|text=Watt might have broken the door down, with an axe, or a crow, or a small '''charge''' of explosive, but this might have aroused Erskine's suspicions, and Watt did not want that.}}
# {{senseid|en|heraldry}} {{lb|en|heraldry}} An image displayed on an [[escutcheon#English:_heraldry|escutcheon]].
#: {{hyper|en|bearing<id:heraldry>}}
#: {{comeronyms|en|field<id:heraldry: background of the shield>}}
#: {{nearsyn|en|emblem<id:heraldry>}}<!--possibly syn or hypo rather than nearsyn-->
# {{lb|en|weaponry}} A position (of a weapon) fitted for attack.
#: {{ux|en|to bring a weapon to the '''charge'''}}
# {{lb|en|farriery}} A sort of plaster or ointment.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Weight; import; value.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Hamlet|V|ii|passage=many suchlike ''as's'' of great '''charge'''}}
# {{lb|en|historical|or|obsolete}} A measure of thirty-six [[pig]]s of [[lead]], each pig weighing about seventy pounds; a {{m|en|charre}}.
# {{lb|en|ecclesiastical}} An address given at a church service concluding a [[visitation]].
# {{lb|en|slang|uncountable}} {{cap|cannabis}}.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1966|author=Alan Bestic|title=Turn Me on Man|page=58
|passage=At about the same time I went off pills and started smoking '''charge''' marijuana, you know.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1970|author=Sean O'Callaghan|title=Drug Addiction in Britain|page=51
|passage=It had been a false alarm, and £2 worth of '''charge''' (marijuana) had gone out of the window.}}

