===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|chanel}} (also as {{m|enm|canel}}, {{m|enm|cannel}}, {{m|enm|kanel}}), a borrowing from {{der|en|fro|chanel}}, {{m|fro|canel}}, from {{der|en|la|canālis|t=groove; canal; channel}}. Doublet of {{m|en|canal}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# The hollow [[bed]] of running waters; {{q|also}} the bed of the [[sea]] or other [[body of water]].
#: {{ux|en|The water coming out of the waterwheel created a standing wave in the '''channel'''.}}
# The natural or [[man-made]] deeper [[course]] through a [[reef]], [[bar]], [[bay]], or any [[shallow]] [[body]] of [[water]].
#: {{ux|en|A '''channel''' was dredged to allow ocean-going vessels to reach the city.}}
#* {{quote-journal| en| date=2013-01| author=Nancy Langston| title=The Fraught History of a Watery World| volume=101| issue=1| page=59| magazine=w:American Scientist| url=http://www.americanscientist.org/bookshelf/pub/the-fraught-history-of-a-watery-world| passage=European adventurers found themselves within a watery world, a tapestry of streams, '''channels''', wetlands, lakes and lush riparian meadows enriched by floodwaters from the Mississippi River.| accessdate=9 May 2013| archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20130122222520/http://www.americanscientist.org/bookshelf/pub/the-fraught-history-of-a-watery-world| archivedate=22 January 2013}}
# The [[navigable]] part of a river.
#: {{ux|en|We were careful to keep our boat in the '''channel'''.}}
# A [[narrow]] body of water between two land masses.
#: {{ux|en|The English '''Channel''' lies between France and England.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2018-05-14| author=Jon Stone in Brussels| magazine=The Independent| url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/politics/brexit-talks-latest-no-progress-negotiations-eu-uk-european-union-theresa-may-a8351411.html| passage=''Our citizens and our businesses on both sides of the '''channel''' need more security and predictability for the future''|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250715080202/https://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/politics/brexit-talks-latest-no-progress-negotiations-eu-uk-european-union-theresa-may-a8351411.html|archivedate=15 July 2025}}
# Something through which another thing passes; a means of conveying or transmitting.
#: {{ux|en|The news was conveyed to us by different '''channels'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Landon Francesca Carrara|pages=69–70|volume=III|text=But I write to tell you what you will hear through all the various '''channels''' by which news travels,...}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1859|author=w:John Call Dalton|title=A Treatise on Human Physiology
|passage=The veins are converging '''channels'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Burke Revolution in France|passage=At best, he is but a '''channel''' to convey to the National Assembly such matter as may import that body to know.}}
# {{lb|en|biochemistry}} An [[ion channel]]: pore-forming [[protein]]s located in a [[cell membrane]] that allow specific [[ion]]s to pass through.
# A gutter; a groove, as in a fluted column.
# {{lb|en|construction|mechanical engineering}} A [[structural]] [[member]] with a cross section shaped like a squared-off letter C.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1960|title=Construction Materials, Concrete Construction, and Engineer Computations|publisher=U.S. Government Printing Office|chapter=Structural Shapes|text=The '''channel''' is not very efficient as a beam or column when used alone, but built-up members may be constructed of '''channels''' assembled together with other structural shapes and connected by rivets or welds.}}
# {{lb|en|electronics}} A [[connection]] between [[initiate|initiating]] and [[terminate|terminating]] [[node]]s of a [[circuit]].
#: {{ux|en|The guard-rail provided the '''channel''' between the downed wire and the tree.}}
# {{lb|en|electronics}} The narrow conducting portion of a [[MOSFET]] transistor.
# {{lb|en|communication}} The part that [[connect]]s a data [[source]] to a data [[sink]].
#: {{ux|en|A '''channel''' stretches between them.}}
# {{lb|en|communication}} A path for [[convey]]ing [[electrical]] or [[electromagnetic]] [[signal]]s, usually distinguished from other [[parallel]] paths.
#: {{ux|en|We are using one of the 24 '''channels'''.}}
# {{lb|en|communication}} A single path provided by a [[transmission]] [[medium]] via physical [[separation]], such as by [[multipair]] cable.
#: {{ux|en|The '''channel''' is created by bonding the signals from these four pairs.}}
# {{lb|en|communication}} A single path provided by a transmission medium via [[spectral]] or [[protocol]] separation, such as by [[frequency]] or [[time-division]] [[multiplexing]].
#: {{ux|en|Their call is being carried on '''channel''' 6 of the T-1 line.}}
# {{lb|en|broadcasting}} A [[specific]] radio frequency or [[band]] of frequencies, usually in conjunction with a predetermined letter, number, or codeword, and allocated by international agreement.
#: {{ux|en|KNDD is the '''channel''' at 107.7 MHz in Seattle.}}
# {{lb|en|broadcasting}} A specific radio frequency or band of frequencies used for [[transmit]]ting [[television]].
#: {{ux|en|NBC is on '''channel''' 11 in San Jose.}}
#* '''2008''', Lou Schuler, "Foreward", ''in'' Nate Green, ''Built for Show'', page xi
#*: TV back then was five '''channels''' (three networks, PBS, and an independent station that ran ''I Love Lucy'' reruns),{{...}}
# {{lb|en|storage}} The portion of a storage medium, such as a [[track]] or a band, that is accessible to a given reading or writing [[station]] or head.
#: {{ux|en|This chip in this disk drive is the '''channel''' device.}}
# The part of a [[turbine]] [[pump]] where the pressure is built up.
#: {{ux|en|The liquid is pressurized in the lateral '''channel'''.}}
# {{lb|en|business|marketing}} A [[distribution channel]].
# {{senseid|en|online conversation area}} {{lb|en|Internet}} A particular area for [[conversation]]s on an [[IRC]] or similar network, analogous to a [[chat room]] and often dedicated to a specific [[topic]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1996|author=Susan C. Herring|title=Computer-mediated Communication|page=50
|passage=The excerpt in Figure 1 below shows a list of some '''channel''' names as they appear to an IRC user. The left hand column displays the '''channel''' name, the middle column displays how many people are currently on the '''channel''', and the right hand column indicates the theme of the '''channel''' or the current topic of conversation: {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|Internet|historical}} A means of delivering [[up-to-date]] [[Internet]] [[content]] via a [[push]] mechanism.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1999|author=Jeffrey S Rule|title=Dynamic HTML: The HTML Developer's Guide
|passage=Netcaster is the "receiver" for '''channels''' that are built into Netscape 4.01 and later releases.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1999|author=Margaret Levine Young|title=Internet: The Complete Reference| passage=To access '''channels''' in Windows 98, you don't have to go any farther than your desktop.}}
# A [[psychic]] or [[medium]] who takes on the [[personality]] of a [[spirit]] and [[convey]]s messages from them.

