===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|chanelen}}, from the noun (see above).

====Verb====
{{en-verb|+l}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To make or cut a channel or [[groove]] in.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[direct]] or guide along a desired course.
#: {{ux|en|We will '''channel''' the traffic to the left with these cones.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1997|author=Eric J Evans|title=Thatcher and Thatcherism
|passage=The resources '''channelled''' to those institutions which showed themselves most adept at formfilling, self-promotion and presentation were inadequate to sustain the quality they claimed.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=03-04-2009|author=Hugh Muir|title=Diary|work=w:The Guardian|url=http://www.guardian.co.uk/politics/2009/apr/03/hugh-muir-labour
|passage=But the real excitement is to be found in Erith and Thamesmead, where the Blairites in exile are '''channelling''' their hopes on Georgia Gould, 22, daughter of Mr Tony's famed pollmeister Philip.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|of a spirit, as of a dead person}} To serve as a [[medium]] for.
#: {{ux|en|She was '''channeling''' the spirit of her late husband, Seth.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To follow as a model, especially in a performance.
#: {{ux|en|He was trying to '''channel''' President Reagan, but the audience wasn't buying it.}}
#: {{ux|en|When it is my turn to sing karaoke, I am going to '''channel''' Ray Charles.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=17-06-2007|author=Bob Morris|title=Mirth, in Honor of Dad|work=w:The New York Times|url=http://www.nytimes.com/2007/06/17/fashion/17age.html
|passage=So I handed out lyrics by Allan Sherman, the shticky 1960s parody writer who my father revered and '''channeled''' to the end.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20240922042727/https://www.nytimes.com/2007/06/17/fashion/17age.html|archivedate=22 September 2024}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=27-04-2008|author=John Mcwhorter|title=Talking the Talk|work=w:The New York Times|url=http://www.nytimes.com/2008/04/27/books/review/McWhorter-t.html
|passage=He is '''channeling''' the most narcotic form of oratory in modern America for whites as well as blacks: a preacherly style of speaking rooted in black American tradition.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20251104234152/https://www.nytimes.com/2008/04/27/books/review/McWhorter-t.html|archivedate=4 November 2025}}

