#: {{ux|en|'''Chancellor''' of the Duchy of Lancaster}}
#: {{ux|en|[[Lord Chancellor|Lord '''Chancellor''']]}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Stephen Vaughan (merchant)|Stephen Vaughan]]|chapter=CCCLXXVI. Vaughan to King [[w:Henry VIII of England|Henry VIII]].|title=State Papers Published under the Authority of Her Majesty’s Commission|location=London|publisher=Printed by [[w:Eyre & Spottiswoode|George E[dward] Eyre and William Spottiswoode]],{{nb...|printers to the Queen’s Most Excellent Majesty.}}|date=6 September 1533|year_published=1849|volume=VII (King Henry the Eighth. Part V.—Continued.)|page=502|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=g8c_AAAAcAAJ&pg=PA502|oclc=614600781|passage=The 6{{sup|th}} daye after, the Duke beyng infourmyd of myne arryvayle, sent his '''Chancellour''' to myne inne, desyryng to knowe the cause of my comying tether;  {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Thomas Rymer; Robert Sanderson|chapter=De Liberatura pro Domino Cancellario Angliæ|title=Fœdera, Conventiones, Literæ, et Cujuscunque Generis Acta Publica, inter Regis Angliæ,{{nb...|et Alios quosvis Imperatores, Reges, Pontifices Principes, vel Communitates: Ab ineunte Sæculo Duodecimo, viz. ab Anno 1101 ad nostra usque Tempora Habita aut Tractata: Ex Autographis, infra Secretiores Archivorum Regiorum Thesaurarias per multa Sæcula reconditis, fideliter Excripta. In lucem missa de Mandato Regio.}}|edition=2nd|location=London|publisher=Per [[w:Jacob Tonson|J[acob] Tonson]]|year=1603|year_published=1727|volume=XVI|page=541|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=9R5aAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA541|column=1|oclc=915435530|passage=We have in the Wiſedome and Dexteritie of, our right truſty and wellbeloved Counſellor, ''[[w:Thomas Egerton, 1st Viscount Brackley|Thomas Lord Elleſmere]]'', and for certen other eſpeciall Cauſes Us moving, have given and graunted unto the ſaid ''Thomas Lord Elleſmere'' the Office of our Lord '''Chauncellor''' of England, and given Aucthority to the ſaid ''Lord Elleſmere'' to heare examyne and determyne Cauſes Matters and Suytes as ſhall happen to bee, as well in our Chauncery as in our Starchamber, like as the '''Chauncellor''' of England or Keeper of the Greate Seale of England,  {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Lettre sur [[w:Johann Fust|Jean Faust]], &c. That is, A Letter Concerning John Faust, Printer of Mentz. By a Librarian of Geneva. Geneva, 1745.|editor=&#91;{{w|Mark Akenside}}&#93;|magazine=The Museum: Or, The Literary and Historical Register|volume=II|issue=XVI|location=London|publisher=Printed for [[w:Robert Dodsley|R[obert] Dodsley]]{{nb...|in Pall-mall.}}|year='''[1746?]''' October 25|year_published=1746 (indicated on title page)|page=106|pageurl=https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=mdp.39015065602057&view=1up&seq=114|oclc=931328825|passage=But tho' this Family might make an honourable Figure at the Bar, yet it ſeems a little unaccountable how a '''Chancellor''' of the ''Bourbonnois'' came to be charg'd with the Reformation of the whole ''French'' Kingdom.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:W. S. Gilbert|W[illiam] S[chwenck] Gilbert]]; {{w|Arthur Sullivan}}, music|title=[[w:Iolanthe|{{...|An Entirely Original Fairy Opera, in Two Acts, Entitled}} Iolanthe; or, The Peer and the Peri]]|location=London|publisher={{w|Chappell & Co.}},{{nb...|50, New Bond Street, W. City Branch—15, Poultry, E.C.}}|year='''1882''' November 25 (first performance)|year_published=[1885?]|section=act I|page=10|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=_9FZAAAAcAAJ&pg=RA10-PA10|oclc=560801973|passage=And everyone who'd marry a Ward / Must come to me for my accord, / And in my court I sit all day, / Giving agreeable girls away, /  {{...}} / And one for thou—and one for thee— / But never, oh never a one for me! / Which is exasperating, for / A highly susceptible '''Chancellor'''!}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:William Searle Holdsworth|W[illiam] S[earle] Holdsworth]]|chapter=English Law in the Sixteenth and Early Seventeenth Centuries (Continued): Developments outside the Sphere of the Common Law (Continued)|title=A History of English Law|location=London|publisher=[[w:Methuen Publishing|Methuen & Co. Ltd.]]{{nb...|36 Essex Street, W.C.}}|year=1923|volume=V|section=part II (The Equitable Jurisdiction of the Chancellor)|pages=235–236|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.31108/page/n265/mode/1up|oclc=1053666846|passage=The '''chancellor''' [''i.e.'', the [[Lord Chancellor]]] is the king's delegate, accountable only to him for his use of his absolute power to purge the defendant's conscience. But we can see in the citation of cases and precedents the influence of the new school of lawyer '''chancellors'''. The usages and customs of the court are a law to the court. It is fairly clear that the process which will reduce to rule and system the occasions upon which the '''chancellor''' will interfere to purge a corrupt conscience, has not as yet gone very far; but we can see in embryo the beginnings of this process.}}
## {{lb|en|UK politics}} {{ellipsis of|en|Chancellor of the Exchequer}}.
##* {{quote-book|en|author=Edward, Earl of Clarendon [''i.e.'', {{w|Edward Hyde, 1st Earl of Clarendon}}]|chapter=Part the Second|title=The Life of Edward, Earl of Clarendon, Lord High Chancellor of England, and Chancellor of the University of Oxford.{{nb...|Containing, I. An Account of the Chancellor’s Life from His Birth to the Restoration in 1660. II. A Continuation of the Same, and of His History of the Grand Rebellion, from the Restoration to His Banishment in 1667. Written by Himself. Printed from His Original Manuscripts Given to the University of Oxford by the Heirs of the Late Earl of Clarendon.}}|location=Oxford, Oxfordshire|publisher=At the [[w:University of Oxford|Clarendon Printing-house]]|year=1760|volume=I|pages=111 and 112|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=26JJAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA111|oclc=723149497|passage=&#91;[https://books.google.com/books?id=26JJAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA111 page 111]&#93;  {{...}} [[w:Henry Cary, 4th Viscount Falkland|Lord ''Falkland'']]  {{...}} took an Opportunity to tell the [[w:Charles II of England|King]], that He had now a good Opportunity to prefer Mr. ''[[w:Edward Hyde, 1st Earl of Clarendon|Hyde]]'', by making him '''Chancellor''' of the Exchequer, in the Place of Sir ''[[w:John Colepeper, 1st Baron Colepeper|John Colepepper]]'';  {{...}} &#91;[https://books.google.com/books?id=26JJAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA112 page 112]&#93; He [Colepepper] ſurrendered his Office of '''Chancellor''' of the Exchequer: And the next Day Mr. ''Hyde'' was ſworn of the Privy-Council, and Knighted, and had his Patents ſealed for that Office.|brackets=on}}
##* {{quote-web|en|title=Philip Hammond: MPs will and should Stop No-deal Brexit|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-politics-48874144|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20190708131132/https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-politics-48874144|archivedate=8 July 2019|work=w:BBC News|date=5 July 2019|passage='''Chancellor''' {{w|Philip Hammond}} has told the BBC he and other MPs will "find a way" of blocking a no-deal Brexit.  {{...}} Mr Hammond is expected to be replaced as '''chancellor''' whoever wins the Conservative leadership election later this month.}}
