# The [[head#Noun|head]] of the government in some [[German#Noun|German]]-[[speak]]ing [[country|countries]].
#: {{synonyms|en|Reichskanzler|q1=historical}}
#: {{ux|en|the Austrian '''Chancellor'''}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=The Scandal in Alsace. Indignation of the Reichstag.|newspaper=[[w:North China Daily News|The North-China Herald and Supreme Court & Consular Gazette: The Weekly Edition of the North-China Daily News]]|location=Shanghai|publisher=Printed and published at the offices of the North-China Daily News & Herald, Ld.|date=13 December 1913|volume=CIX (New Series)|issue=2418|page=783|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=VZYhAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA783|column=1|oclc=662525861|passage=In the Reichstag, the Imperial '''Chancellor''', Dr. [[w:Theobald von Bethmann-Hollweg|[Theobald] von Bethmann-Hollweg]], replied to questions upon the [[w:Zabern Affair|Zabern incident]] in a tone of protest and anger which has not been heard since the Morocco debate. Half the House itself was seething with indignation against the Government.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2003|author=Owen Chadwick|title=A History of the Popes, 1830-1914|publisher=Oxford University Press, USA|isbn=9780199262861|page=194
|text=He was a moderate who wanted German unity and became '''Chancellor''' of Bavaria after the Prussian defeat of Austria.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|title=Angela Merkel to Step down as German Chancellor in 2021|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-europe-46020745|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20190630142305/https://www.bbc.com/news/world-europe-46020745|archivedate=30 June 2019|work=w:BBC News|date=29 October 2018|passage=Germany's {{w|Angela Merkel}} has said she will step down as '''chancellor''' in 2021, following recent election setbacks. "I will not be seeking any political post after my term ends," she told a news conference in Berlin.}}
# {{lb|en|Christianity}} A senior [[record keeper]] of a [[cathedral]]; a senior legal officer for a [[bishop]] or [[diocese]] in charge of [[hear]]ing [[case#Noun|cases]] involving [[ecclesiastical]] [[law]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=W. T. P.|title=The Consistory Court of St. Asaph|magazine=[[w:Bye-gones|Bye-gones Relating to Wales and the Border Counties]]|location=Oswestry, Shropshire; Wrexham, Denbighshire|publisher=Woodall, Minshall, Thomas and Co.; London: {{w|Elliot Stock}}{{nb...|62, Paternoster Row, E.C.}}|date=20 November 1907|volume=X (New Series)|page=139|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=f4wVAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA138|oclc=151039813|passage=The '''Chancellor''', as he is commonly called, who presides in the Diocesan Court, is appointed by the Bishop to the two ancient offices of Vicar General and Official Principal. When the Bishop, as was frequently the case, was absent from the Diocese, or for any other reason was unable to act in person, the Vicar General, as the name denotes, was his usual representative, while the exercise of his judicial authority he delegated to the Official Principal. For a long period of time the two offices have been always held together, and the '''Chancellor''' sometimes acts in one capacity and sometimes in the other.}}
# {{lb|en|education}} The head of a [[university]], sometimes purely [[ceremonial#Adjective|ceremonial]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Frederick Douglas How|title=The Marquis of Salisbury|editor=Donald Macleod|magazine=w:Good Words|location=London|publisher=Isbister and Company Limited{{nb...|15 and 16 Tavistock Street Covent Garden}}|year=1902|section=section II (Oxford Days)|pages=141–142|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=rdURAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA141|oclc=611177933|passage=When the extremely arduous duties of the Prime Minister’s &#91;{{w|Robert Gascoyne-Cecil, 3rd Marquess of Salisbury}}’s&#93; political life are considered, it will be understood that, if the Chancellorship of the University entailed any serious amount of work, it would have been impossible for him to continue in the office. As a matter of fact, this is not the case. There are '''Chancellor'''’s Prizes which he gives, but he does not present them in person. There is a '''Chancellor'''’s Court to which University men may be summoned, but it is invariably presided over by the Vice-Chancellor, who is, in fact, the one really executive authority. Sometimes the '''Chancellor''' heads deputations to Court, and sometimes (but rarely) he comes to [the [[w:University of Oxford|University of] Oxford]] to preside over some special function, when his weighty words are greatly valued.}}
# {{lb|en|Scots law}} The [[foreman]] of a [[jury]].
#* {{RQ:Scott Tales of My Landlord 2|volume=II|chapter=XII|pages=285–286|pageref=285|passage="Have you agreed on your '''chancellor''', gentlemen?" was the first question of the Judge. The foreman, called in Scotland the '''chancellor''' of the jury, usually the man of best rank and estimation among the assizers, stepped forward, and, with a low reverence, delivered to the Court a sealed paper, containing the verdict,  {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Robert Bell; William Bell|entry=CRIMINAL PROSECUTION|title=A Dictionary of the Law of Scotland|edition=3rd revised and enlarged|location=Edinburgh|publisher=Printed for John Anderson & Co.{{nb...|Royal Exchange}}, and Bell & Bradfute|year=1826|volume=I|page=333|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=e1dHAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA334|oclc=964339456|passage=The verdict must be returned to the Court by the '''chancellor''' of the jury, in presence of the accused, and of the whole jury; and, being engrossed in the record, and read aloud, it is then sealed up, in terms of the regulations 1672, No. 9, and deposited with the clerk of Court, never to be opened again but by order of the judges. The verdict, when in writing, is authenticated by the subscriptions of the '''chancellor''' and clerk of the jury, and accompanied with a list of the names of the jurors, and a state of the vote of each individual, "whether condemning or assoilzieing;" ''Regulations'' 1672, No. 9.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Geo[rge] Dingwall Fordyce (reporter); James Paterson (reporter); William Gillies Tytler (reporter); {{w|Frederick Hallard}} (reporter)|title={{smallcaps|James Mackenzie}}, ''Pursuer, v.'' {{smallcaps|Dunlop, Wilson}} and Co., ''Defenders.''|journal=Reports of Cases Decided in the Supreme Courts of Scotland, and in the House of Lords on Appeal from Scotland,{{nb...|[...] Being a Continuation of The Scottish Jurist.}}|location=Edinburgh|publisher=M. Anderson and Co.,{{nb...|Law-printers, North Hanover Street.}}|date=16 July 1853|volume=XXV|page=558|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=zf5CAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA558|column=2|oclc=|passage=[T]he pursuer moved the Court "for a rule to shew cause why this case should not be ordered to be tried, in respect that the verdict as it appears on the notes of the Judge furnished to the parties, was not declared by the '''chancellor''' or foreman of the jury in open Court, and taken down by the clerk of the said Court before the jury was discharged."}}
# {{lb|en|US|law}} The [[chief#Adjective|chief]] [[judge#Noun|judge]] of a [[court#Noun|court]] of [[chancery]] (that is, one [[exercise#Verb|exercising]] [[equity]] [[jurisdiction]]).
#* {{quote-book|en|chapter=Virginia State Law and Regulations|editor=[[w:William Griffith (New Jersey attorney)|William Griffith]]|title=Annual Law Register of the United States|location=Burlington, N.J.|publisher=David Allinson|year=1821–1822|year_published=1822|volume=III|section=paragraph VI (The “Superiour Courts of Chancery”)|page=321|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=RCtOAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA321|column=1|oclc=639956272|passage=The state [of Virginia, USA] is divided into 9 chancery districts, in each of which a ''superiour court of chancery'' is held. There are 4 '''chancellors'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:John Bouvier|entry=CHANCERY|title=[[w:Bouvier's Law Dictionary|A Law Dictionary, Adapted to the Constitution and Laws of the United States of America,{{nb...|and of the Several States of the American Union: With References to the Civil and Other Systems of Foreign Law. To which is Added Kelham’s Dictionary of the Norman and Old French Language.}}]]|edition=11th|location=Philadelphia, Pa.|publisher=[[w:George William Childs|George W[illiam] Childs]],{{nb...|628 & 630 Chestnut St.}}|year=1864|volume=I|section=paragraph 3|page=219|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=YOVRAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA219|column=2|oclc=680529889|passage=In some of the states, as New York, Virginia, and South Carolina, the equity court is a distinct tribunal, having its appropriate judge, or '''chancellor''', and officers.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|chapter=John Blair: Appointment as Associate Justice in 1789|editors=w:Maeva Marcus; James R. Perry|title=The Documentary History of the Supreme Court of the United States, 1789–1800|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Columbia University Press|year=1985|volume=1, part 1 (Appointments and Proceedings)|page=54|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=gpmvcaCIukcC&pg=PA54|isbn=978-0-231-08867-1|passage=[[w:John Blair Jr.|[John] Blair [Jr.]]] began his long judicial career when the state legislature—following the establishment of Virginia's judicial department in October, 1777—elected him one of five judges of the newly organized General Court. By 1779 he had become chief justice of that court, and, in November, 1780, he became '''chancellor''' of the three-member High Court of Chancery.}}
