From {{inh|en|enm|chalis}}, from {{der|en|xno|-}}, from {{der|en|fro|chalice}}, collateral form of {{m|fro|calice}}, borrowed from {{der|en|la|calix|calix, calicem|t=cup}}, of uncertain etymology. In view of {{cog|xum|skalçeta||sacrifical vessel}}, perhaps from a {{der|en|itc-pro||*(s)kalik-}},<ref name="EDL">{{R:itc:EDL|head=calix, -icis|pages=83–84}}</ref> from {{der|en|ine-pro|*(s)kel-}}. Pokorny considered a parallel formation in {{cog|sa|कलश|tr=kaláśa-|t=(water-)jar, tub, pot, dish}}, for {{m+|ine-pro||*kel-eḱ-}},<ref>{{R:ine:IEW|head=7. ''kel-'' (''kol-'', ''k{{sub|o}}l-'')|pages=550–551}}</ref> but De Vaan finds this unlikely. Alternatively, borrowed from {{der|en|grc|κύλιξ}} or an unattested variant thereof, maybe with contamination from {{m|grc|κάλυξ||shell, [[calyx]]}}, but it is also possible that all were borrowed from related {{der|en|qfa-sub}} words.<ref name="EDL"/> Possible {{doublet|en|calyx|kelch|nocap=1}}. Compare Sumerian [[𒃲]](GAL).

